 A4 – Design Dynamic Memory Use

A4.1 – Growable Donor List

```text
IF size == capacity THEN
    newCapacity = capacity * 2
    IF capacity == 0 THEN
        newCapacity = 2
    END IF

    p = realloc(data, newCapacity * sizeof(Donor))

    IF p == NULL THEN
        RETURN FAILURE
    END IF

    data = p
    capacity = newCapacity
END IF

data[size] = donor
size = size + 1
RETURN SUCCESS
```

The list starts with a small capacity and doubles whenever it becomes full. `realloc()` increases memory dynamically.

### A4.2 – Memory Errors

* Memory leak: Allocated memory is not freed. Prevented by calling `free()` when the list is no longer needed.

* Dangling pointer: A pointer refers to freed memory. Prevented by setting the pointer to `NULL` after freeing.

* Double free: The same memory is freed twice. Prevented by freeing memory only once.

### Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis

If the capacity increases by 10 records each time, the array will grow slowly. It needs more `realloc()` calls and copying.

| Method          | Total copying | Unused space       |
| --------------- | ------------- | ------------------ |
| Double capacity | O(n)          | Less than capacity |
| Increase by 10  | O(n²)         | Maximum 9 records  |

Doubling capacity is faster for large donor lists because it reduces the number of memory reallocations.
